{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoBranchByArgument","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoBranchByArgument","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoEnvelopeAssembly","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Complex.Log"],"declarations":4,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoDeformedSaddle.branchInt_hypotheses_satisfiable","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoDeformedSaddle.branchInt_hypotheses_satisfiable","kind":"theorem","line":115,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoBranchByArgument.lean#L115-L127","doc":"**Non-vacuity.** The hypotheses of `branchInt_eq_zero_of_arg_structure` are\nsatisfiable, with the measured arc values: taking `a₁ = 1.4`, `a₂ = a₃ = 0`\nand `φ = 1.4` gives a genuine instance, so the theorem is not about an empty set.\n\nThe check with real content is Route 27, which measures the three structural\nfacts along the arc and finds the margins `−1.4077` vs `−π/2` and `1.3099` vs\n`π/2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoDeformedSaddle.branchInt_eq_zero_of_arg_structure","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoDeformedSaddle.branchInt_eq_zero_of_arg_structure","kind":"theorem","line":99,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoBranchByArgument.lean#L99-L111","doc":"**The replacement for `hcont`, assembled.**\n\nGiven the argument bounds and `φ ∈ [0,π)`, the branch integer vanishes. Compare\n`branchDiff_eq_zero_of_continuousOn`, which reaches the same conclusion from\ncontinuity plus a base point: this route needs neither. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoDeformedSaddle.sum_bounds_of_arg_structure","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoDeformedSaddle.sum_bounds_of_arg_structure","kind":"theorem","line":86,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoBranchByArgument.lean#L86-L97","doc":"The bounds, from the structure of the three individual arguments.\n\n`a₁` is the one that may exceed `π/2` (at most one does); `a₂`, `a₃` stay at or\nbelow it; all three exceed `−π/2`; and at least one is nonnegative. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoDeformedSaddle.branchInt_eq_zero_of_bounds","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoDeformedSaddle.branchInt_eq_zero_of_bounds","kind":"theorem","line":72,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoBranchByArgument.lean#L72-L84","doc":"**The branch integer is `0` as soon as the argument sum is bounded.**\n\nIf `S = φ + 2π·k` with `S ∈ (−π, 2π)` and `φ ∈ [0, π)`, then `|2πk| < 2π`, so\n`k = 0`. Pure arithmetic — no continuity, no base point, no topology. ","signatureChunk":0}]}